    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Malay is an Austronesian language whose ancestor, Proto-Malayic, was spoken in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> at least by 1000 BCE; its oldest record is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kedukan-bukit-inscription">Kedukan Bukit inscription</a> of 683 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of traders; under the Malacca Sultanate (1402–1511) it took in Arabic words with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and became Classical Malay, the language of courts and letters.</li><li>Written first in Indian-derived scripts, then in Jawi and now mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script, Malay is spoken by close to 300 million people as Standard Malay and as Indonesian.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Among the oldest surviving letters in Malay are those Sultan Abu Hayat of Ternate sent to the king of Portugal around 1521–1522, although the Ternateans spoke the unrelated Ternate language and used Malay only as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

